messages:
Anna K: FYI I'm seeing the onboarding sampleapp blow up under burst starts — a bunch of 429s then an OOM. Repro attached below.

Anna K: Repro steps: start 50 parallel clients against local sampleapp, each client paginates through our dataset and does an embed + generation per page. Using JS SDK v0.8.3 with default settings.

Anna K: Minimal snippet from the sampleapp that I think matters:
```js
// naive concurrency control in sampleapp
const sem = new Semaphore(100);
async function fetchPage(cursor) {
  await sem.acquire();
  try {
    const token = await auth.getToken(); // might refresh
    const res = await sdk.generate({ token, cursor });
    return res;
  } finally {
    sem.release();
  }
}
```

Ravi S: logs? can you paste a snippet of the 429 + OOM trace? :eyes:

Anna K: log excerpt:
```
2026-03-22T18:04:12Z ERROR request failed status=429 retry-after=1 body="{...}": LLM request failed
2026-03-22T18:04:18Z ERROR uncaught exception: RangeError: Invalid array length
    at KVCache._alloc (kvcache.js:42:12)
    at processTicksAndRejections (internal/process/task_queues.js:93:5)
```

Mei L: This looks like a classic token-refresh stampede + overflowing KV cache from many parallel sequences. If auth.getToken triggers refresh inside each concurrent request you get a lot of duplicate work and spikes in downstream load.

Mei L: Two quick mitigations: (1) make token refresh atomic (single-flight) at client level, (2) cap active sequences per client with a small default (eg 12) and provide an opt-in higher concurrency for dedicated customers.

Carlos G: Agree. In JS SDK we recently landed a small change to cache the refresh promise. Something like:
```js
async getToken() {
  if (this._refreshPromise) return this._refreshPromise;
  if (this._isExpired()) {
    this._refreshPromise = this._doRefresh();
    try {
      return await this._refreshPromise;
    } finally {
      this._refreshPromise = null;
    }
  }
  return this._cachedToken;
}
```

Ravi S: Nice — that prevents N refreshes. We should also add jittered backoff on the client side to smooth bursts (randomize retry wait).

Anna K: If we do that + reduce semaphore default from 100 to 12, the sampleapp stops hitting OOM in my quick runs. But it hurts throughput for big customers. We need a balanced default + docs.

Mei L: SDK parity note: JS change is small, but Go and Python SDKs currently refresh per request in sampleapp examples. We should standardize pattern across SDKs and put a one-liner in onboarding that says "set maxConcurrentSequences or rely on client default".

Carlos G: For parity I'll open a PR to go-sdk with same single-flight refresh + optional Config.MaxConcurrentSequences (default 12). Will also add a sampleapp flag `--burst-safe` that toggles these safer defaults.

Anna K: Also found one subtle bug: our paginator retries on 429 by immediately re-issuing request without respecting per-client concurrency slots, so retries pile up. We should queue retries behind the same sem rather than spin them.

Ravi S: Good catch. Implementation idea: retry wrapper should await sem.acquire() before retry attempt and use exponential backoff + jitter.

Mei L: Tests: add an integration harness that simulates token expiry + N parallel clients and asserts no more than 1 refresh in X seconds and memory usage stays bounded. I can write the harness.

Anna K: TL;DR action items:
- Carlos: PR for Go SDK parity (single-flight + MaxConcurrentSequences)
- Mei: integration harness + sampleapp test case
- Ravi: add docs blurb + recommended defaults for hosted vs dedicated
- Anna: update sampleapp to use `--burst-safe` flag and include reproduction script in repo

Carlos G: I'll target a PR by EOD tomorrow.

onboard-bot: Created task #1427: "SDK: single-flight token refresh and client concurrency defaults" assigned to Carlos G, Mei L, Ravi S.

Ravi S: After we land SDK changes we should also ship a CLI check during onboarding that warns if sampleapp is run with >50 concurrency and not in `--burst-safe` mode. Saves people from footguns. :thumbsup:

Anna K: Sounds good. Thanks all — will follow up with the repro and a small flame graph showing where memory spikes happen.

Mei L: :100:
